<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-hannezo-branching-random-walk-annihilation-specimen-match> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Hannezo et al. (2017) Cell & Scheele et al. (2017) Nature unifying theory of branching morphogenesis via branching and annihilating random walks (BARW) — Morphological realism / specimen match: 84/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "The SHA-256-verified specimen (`mouse_branched_ductal_network_specimen.jpg`) is segmented into active buds and EDT medial-axis junctions (L706-806), recovering N_term = 34, N_branch = 33 and the exact binary-tree identity, and Panel F shows the segmentation beside the simulated trees; the Pass 3 3D fat-pad and clonal panels (G, I) visibly resemble the cited ductal trees and lineage mosaics. Deductions: the specimen is a Wikimedia whole-mount of an embryonic branched gland (salivary/lung-like acinar buds), not the pubertal mammary TEB network or the kidney/pancreas organs the model targets, and no figure from Hannezo, Scheele or Bordeu is placed beside the simulation (-8); the reference values N_term = 34 and r/R_max = 0.783 (L1917, L1927) are the segmentation's own outputs from a tuned size-14 maximum filter / edt > 4 threshold, so the specimen metrics are circular (-5); the WT 2D tree in Panels A and I has only 13 terminals, far sparser than any specimen (-3). Not changed in Pass 3; the v2 score of 90 did not account for the organ mismatch." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/concept/morphoeval-0.1.0-specimen-match> ;
    pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/model/gemini-4-argon> ;
